{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788307499,"nonce":"+if2zuw6ljYM82HBSIqmMmqhUyU=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPxSuQ","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/31","origin":"github"},"title":"bencode.py cannot decode v2/hybrid torrents, and masks the reason","message":"Running libtorrent's 108-torrent corpus through the current parsing path, **34 fail to decode**, including every BitTorrent v2 and hybrid torrent:\n\n```\nok                                  50\nbdecode raises BencodeDecodeError   34\nparses but escapes output dir        9\npath/size handling raises KeyError   9\npath/size handling raises TypeError  3\ninfo not a dict                      1\nbdecode raises TypeError             1\n```\n\nThe failures include `v2.torrent`, `v2_hybrid.torrent`, `v2_only.torrent`, `v2_multiple_files.torrent` and ~30 other v2 files, plus `empty-files-1` through `empty-files-5`, which are v2 torrents used for zero-length file tests.\n\n**The error message is useless for diagnosis.** `bencodepy.decoder.decode` wraps everything:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    value = to_binary(value)\n    data, length = self.decode_func[value[0:1]](value, 0)\nexcept (IndexError, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):\n    raise BencodeDecodeError(\"not a valid bencoded string\")\n```\n\nSo a genuinely corrupt torrent, an unsupported-but-valid v2 torrent, and an internal bug in the decoder are indistinguishable to the caller. There is no way to tell a user \"this is a v2 torrent, which is not supported yet\" rather than \"this file is broken\".\n\nRuled out while investigating: zero-length bencoded strings decode fine (`bdecode(b\"d0:i1ee\")` works), so that is not the cause.\n\nRelevant to the parser work in #19 and to the rewrite in #28. Writing our own decoder would fix the diagnosis problem for free, since it can report what and where, and it is a prerequisite for v2 support either way.","files":null}]}